# Current state

The survey paints a clear picture: documentation is considered important, but it is rarely maintained. Only one in three respondents believes their own documentation is up to date.

{% figure src="../assets/chart.svg" caption="Share of up-to-date documentation by way of working (n = 214)" alt="Bar chart: word processor 31%, wiki 46%, docs as code 78%." width="85%" /%}

## The most common obstacles

| Obstacle | Respondents |
|:---|---:|
| No time for maintenance alongside development | 62% |
| Unclear who is responsible for which content | 48% |
| Multiple versions in different locations | 41% |
| Time-consuming formatting for PDF and web | 37% |

One finding stands out – the link between how teams work and how current their documentation is: teams that maintain documentation in the repository update it more than twice as often. The main reason is that changes to the product and to its documentation are reviewed in **one** step.
